---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import heightData from '../../../data/percentiles/height.json';
import validation from '../../../data/validation.json';

/**
 * De methodepagina. Beide metriekpagina's verwijzen hierheen voor het volledige
 * verhaal. Elk getal komt uit de gegenereerde databestanden of uit
 * data/validation.json, dat de validatiesuite bij elke geslaagde run wegschrijft.
 */

const count = (n) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const measuredAdults = heightData.sex.male.respondents + heightData.sex.female.respondents;

const title = 'How We Calculate Percentiles | HumanAverage';
const description =
  'Our percentiles come from NHANES survey microdata, weighted and smoothed, not copied ' +
  'from published averages. Here is the full method, the checks we run, and the limits.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical="/method/">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1>How we get these numbers</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Anyone can repeat an average. The reason this site exists is that we start one step
        earlier — from the individual measurements — and do the arithmetic ourselves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow content">
      <h2>Averages versus microdata</h2>
      <p>
        A published statistic like “the average American man is 5 feet 9 inches” is the end of
        a calculation. Someone measured thousands of people, weighted them, and reduced all of
        it to one number. Everything that made the answer interesting — the spread, the shape,
        how it changes with age — was thrown away at that last step.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Microdata is the row-by-row file that existed before that summary.</strong> One
        line per person: age, sex, measured height, measured weight, and the statistical weight
        that says how many Americans that person represents. With those rows you can answer a
        question no summary can: not “what is typical”, but “where does this specific person
        fall”. That is the only reason a percentile on this site can exist.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES)</strong>, run by
        the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. It is unusual among large surveys
        in one respect that matters enormously here: participants are <em>measured</em> by
        trained staff in a mobile examination centre. They are not asked what they weigh.
        Self-reported height and weight are known to be biased, and a percentile built on them
        would inherit that bias.
      </p>
      <p>
        We use two cycles — 2017 to March 2020, and August 2021 to August 2023 — giving
        {count(measuredAdults)} measured adults between 20 and 79. The files are in the public
        domain, and our pipeline records the exact download URLs and a checksum for each, so a
        silent change at the source makes the build fail rather than quietly shift the numbers.
      </p>

      <h2>What we do to it</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Apply the survey weights.</strong> NHANES deliberately over-samples some
          groups so it can report on them reliably. Every participant therefore carries a factor
          representing how many Americans they stand for. Ignoring it is not a small
          simplification: for men aged 20 and over, the unweighted average height is 173.4 cm
          while the correctly weighted figure is 175.2 cm. Nearly two centimetres, from one
          skipped step.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Combine the two cycles.</strong> One covers about 3.2 years, the other 2, so
          each is scaled to its share of the combined period. For equal-length cycles this is
          exactly the standard practice of dividing by the number of cycles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Smooth across age.</strong> There are roughly a hundred people per single year
          of age — plenty for a median, too few for a stable 90th percentile. So each age draws
          on its neighbours, weighted by how close they are, before the percentiles are
          calculated. Every age we publish rests on at least {validation.thinnestCellRespondents}
          effective respondents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Drop the top-coded ages.</strong> NHANES records everyone aged 80 and over as
          simply “80”. That bin holds people from 80 to past 100, so it is not an age at all. We
          exclude it rather than present it as one, and publish ages 20 to 79.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How we check ourselves</h2>
      <p>
        A pipeline that reads a binary survey file and applies statistical weights has plenty of
        room to be subtly, invisibly wrong. So before anything is published, we recompute the
        summary statistics that NCHS publishes itself and require an exact match.
      </p>

      <div class="data-table-wrap">
        <table class="data-table">
          <caption>Our calculation against the published NCHS figures, {validation.cycle}</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Measure</th>
              <th scope="col">Group</th>
              <th scope="col">We calculate</th>
              <th scope="col">NCHS publishes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {validation.reference.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.measure}</th>
                <td>{row.group}</td>
                <td>{row.computed} → {row.rounded} {row.unit}</td>
                <td>{row.published} {row.unit}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        All four reproduce the published figures exactly. Two further checks run alongside them:
        the smoothed curves are compared against raw percentiles computed directly in wide age
        bands, where the largest disagreement is {validation.smoothingMaxDeviationCm} cm, and the
        decoded body-mass values are checked against weight divided by height squared. If any
        check fails, the build stops.
      </p>
      <p class="field-hint">
        Reference figures: {validation.citation} Checked {validation.checkedOn}.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will not do</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>No percentile without microdata underneath it.</strong> Plenty of sites will
          tell you that your reaction time beats 85% of people. Ask where that distribution came
          from and there is usually no answer. We only show a percentile where a representative,
          measured sample exists — which is why our height and weight tools have one, and why
          the older reaction-time and memory tests on this domain never will.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Limits you should know</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>These are American percentiles.</strong> NHANES samples the U.S. civilian
          population. Body size differs substantially between countries, so if you live
          elsewhere your result says where you would fall among Americans.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ages 20 to 79.</strong> Younger ages belong to growth charts, which are a
          different instrument with different rules. Older ages are top-coded in the source.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sex at birth, two categories.</strong> That is what the survey records. We
          would rather state the limit than invent groups the data cannot support.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A snapshot, not a life story.</strong> The survey measures different people at
          different ages at one moment in time. When the curve falls after middle age, that mixes
          two things — people do lose height as they age, and people born decades apart grew up
          under different conditions. A cross-sectional survey cannot separate them, and neither
          can we.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not a health assessment.</strong> A percentile describes how common your
          measurement is. It says nothing about whether it is good for you.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="source-note">
        Source: Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), National Center for Health
        Statistics (NCHS). National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, 2017–March 2020 and
        August 2021–August 2023. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. Data retrieved
        30 August 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .field-hint { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.9rem; }
    .content ol { display: grid; gap: 14px; }
  </style>
</Base>
